# Brewfile-core - Declarative Homebrew package management
#                 Entries here should work on both macOS and Linux.
#
# There is no plain `Brewfile`. Packages are split across:
#   Brewfile-core   - cross-platform (this file)
#   Brewfile-mac    - macOS only (casks, Mac App Store apps, Mac formulae)
#   Brewfile-linux  - Linux only
#
# Usage:
# 1. Sync packages - previews changes, confirms, then installs what's
#    missing and removes what isn't listed:
#    `~/bin/bundle-sync-brewfile.sh`
# 2. Sync VS Code extensions - not covered by step 1:
#    `~/bin/vscode-sync-extensions.sh --preview`   (dry-run)
#    Every `vscode` entry is currently commented out in both files, so a run
#    without `--preview` would uninstall every installed extension. Uncomment
#    the ones to keep before running it for real.
#
# Notes:
# - `brew` entries are command-line utilities.
# - `cask` entries are macOS GUI applications.
# - `mas` entries are Mac App Store applications.
# - `vscode` entries install VS Code extensions.
# - Both scripts concatenate this file with the OS-specific one. Running
#   `brew bundle` against a single file directly will see only half the
#   packages and offer to uninstall the rest.
# - The sync passes `--no-upgrade`: it installs what's missing but does not
#   upgrade what's already present. Use `brew upgrade` for that.
# - On Linux, `cask` and `mas` lines are stripped automatically.
# - `brew bundle dump` writes one flat file and has no idea about this
#   split, so it will clobber the layout. Add entries by hand.
#
# Warning: syncing removes anything installed but not listed here
# (except VS Code extensions, which `brew bundle` ignores).

